s towards  growth and and widely shared prosperity  rather than  emphasizing that debt in itself is it  one that's working in both developed and  developing countries  well the important thing to think i  think about um  about the difference between the global  north and the global south countries  like germany and the uk  versus countries such as india  bangladesh  cambodia is that in many cases those  debts aren't held in their national  currency so that puts a very different  spin on things essentially  so whereas we are able to control the  terms of our debt in a country in the  global south you tend to  hold debt for example in u.s dollars or  widely available  uh currencies such as pound sterling and  as a result of that that means you don't  have that control over the terms by  which you repay that debt  now this means that you have to repay it  even if it is economically extremely  damaging to do so  and so this is by no means a new issue  it's something which has been going on  for a very long time but it is likely to  be accelerated in a very significant  way by the debts of crude as a result of  the coveted 19 crisis  just to give you some idea of the extent  to which this already goes on  just the interest payments alone from  banks in new york and london have  amounted to around 4.2 trillion  in payments from the global south to  global north and that is already  considerably more than the total sum of  development aid spending so in many ways  we have a situation where as a result of  debt  it's the global south developing the  global north rather than the other way  around  um so a lot of this depends upon what  the dollar is doing what the us economy  is doing  let's look at the total public debt in  the u.s as a percentage of gross  domestic product  from the global financial crisis up  until this crisis  the graph gets scary how are we going to  keep up or how is the us going to keep  up  well i mean the us has been the holders  of one of the major